        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Scientific Revolution</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Universities, courts and cities across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> to the German lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, linked by printed books, letters and, later, scientific societies</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1543–1687 by a common convention, from Copernicus’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">On the Revolutions</a> to Newton’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a>; often taken more broadly as c. 1500–1700</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes">René Descartes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-boyle">Robert Boyle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">A Sun-centred cosmos governed by universal mathematical laws, the experimental method, and lasting scientific institutions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> and the Académie des sciences</span></div>
