    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>From Copernicus in 1543 to Newton in 1687, European scholars replaced the Earth-centred cosmos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s physics with a Sun-centred system governed by mathematical laws.</li><li>Its people joined mathematics, measurement and experiment, used new instruments such as the telescope, microscope and air pump, and shared their results in books, letters, journals and academies.</li><li>Historians debate how revolutionary it was: it built on Greek, Islamic and medieval learning, and the name itself became common only in the 20th century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Newton’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> was printed in 1687 at the expense of Edmond Halley, partly because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> had spent its funds on an expensive illustrated History of Fishes by Francis Willughby and John Ray.</p></aside>
    </section>

