    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1543</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> publishes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">On the Revolutions</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-vesalius">Andreas Vesalius</a> publishes On the Fabric of the Human Body</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1572</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a> observes a new star and shows that it lies beyond the Moon, in heavens thought unchangeable</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1609</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>’s New Astronomy shows that Mars moves in an ellipse around the Sun</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1610</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidereus-nuncius">Starry Messenger</a> reports mountains on the Moon and four moons of Jupiter</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1628</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-harvey">William Harvey</a> shows that the blood circulates, pumped by the heart</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1633</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a> condemns <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> for defending the motion of the Earth</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1660</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> is founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1687</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> publishes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a>, with the laws of motion and universal gravitation</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy.jpg" alt="Claudius Ptolemy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy">Claudius Ptolemy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_1476_with_armillary_sphere_model.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Justus van Gent / Pedro Berruguete, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For nearly two thousand years learned people around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> understood nature through the works of Greek thinkers, copied and commented on in Greek, Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> taught that the Earth stood still at the centre of a finite universe, that everything below the Moon was made of four elements, each moving naturally towards its own place, and that the heavens were made of a fifth, unchanging substance turning in perfect circles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy">Claudius Ptolemy</a>, written in the 2nd century CE, gave astronomers a geometrical model of the planets, built of circles upon circles, that predicted their positions well, and the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> governed anatomy and medicine. From the 13th century these authors formed the core of teaching in the universities of Europe.</p><p>Much of this learning had reached Latin Europe through the Islamic world. Scholars writing in Arabic, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, translated, criticised and extended Greek science, and in the 12th century translators such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona">Gerard of Cremona</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> turned Arabic texts into Latin, among them the Almagest and many works of Arabic science. Ibn al-Haytham’s Book of Optics, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in the early 11th century, shaped European optics into the 17th century. Astronomers at the observatory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maragha">Maragha</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, founded in 1259, and Ibn al-Shatir in 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> built planetary models without the equant, the device of Ptolemy that philosophers found hardest to accept, and some of their geometrical techniques reappear in Copernicus, though how he came to know them is debated. Medieval Latin scholars questioned Aristotle too: in 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Jean Buridan explained continued motion by an impressed force, impetus, and Nicole Oresme discussed whether the Earth might turn daily on its axis.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and the age of European voyages unsettled this inheritance. Humanists printed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a> and Ptolemy in Greek, and the recovery of rival ancient schools, such as the atomism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretius">Lucretius</a>, showed that the ancients had disagreed among themselves; voyagers found lands, peoples, plants and animals unknown to the ancients; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> put accurate drawings, maps and tables into many identical copies. Practical needs, in navigation, gunnery, mining, surveying and the reform of the calendar carried out in 1582, rewarded skill in mathematics and in the making of instruments.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rene-descartes.jpg" alt="René Descartes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes">René Descartes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frans_Hals_-_Portret_van_Ren%C3%A9_Descartes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Frans Hals, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The people of the Scientific Revolution did not call themselves scientists, a word coined in the 1830s, but natural philosophers, astronomers, physicians and mathematicians, and they did not share a single programme. What many of them sought was a knowledge of nature more certain and more useful than the commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> taught in the universities: one founded on observation and measurement, tested by experiment and, where possible, expressed in mathematics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> wrote in The Assayer (1623) that the great book of the universe “is written in the language of mathematics”. Francis Bacon, in his New Organon (1620), called instead for the patient collection of facts and experiments, from which general laws would be reached step by step, and for knowledge that would improve human life.</p><p>Many hoped to explain nature as a machine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes">René Descartes</a>, in his Discourse on the Method (1637) and Principles of Philosophy (1644), described the physical world as matter in motion acting only by contact, and rejected the qualities and purposes of Aristotle’s physics; Pierre Gassendi revived the ancient atomism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-boyle">Robert Boyle</a> joined such ideas of tiny particles to experiment. This mechanical philosophy promised explanations that anyone could follow and test. The new experimental philosophy also had social rules. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>, whose motto Nullius in verba means “on no one’s word”, experiments were performed before witnesses and reported in plain prose so that others could repeat them.</p><p>For most of them religion was not an opponent. Copernicus was a canon of a cathedral chapter, Kepler saw his laws as the geometry by which God had built the world, Boyle wrote in defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and left money for lectures to defend it, and Newton wrote more on theology and the interpretation of prophecy than on physics. They argued, rather, about how far scripture and the authority of the Church should govern the reading of nature, and that argument came to a head in the trial of Galileo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidereus-nuncius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sidereus-nuncius.jpg" alt="Sidereus Nuncius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sidereus-nuncius">Sidereus Nuncius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houghton_IC6.G1333.610sa_-_Sidereus_nuncius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Galileo Galilei, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Two books published in 1543 came to mark its beginning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, a canon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frombork">Frombork</a> in Warmia, published <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres</a>, which placed the Sun near the centre of the universe and made the Earth a planet, turning daily on its axis and circling the Sun each year; an unsigned preface by the Lutheran theologian Andreas Osiander presented the book as a hypothesis for calculation. In the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-vesalius">Andreas Vesalius</a>, professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, published On the Fabric of the Human Body, whose detailed woodcuts, based on his own dissections, corrected many of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>’s errors. In 1572 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a> observed a brilliant new star and showed that it lay beyond the Moon, and in 1577 that a comet did too, so that the heavens were neither unchanging nor made of solid spheres. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a>, his observatory on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ven-island">Hven</a>, he made the most accurate observations recorded before the telescope.</p><p>Tycho’s records passed to his assistant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>, who showed in his New Astronomy (1609) that Mars moves not in a circle but in an ellipse with the Sun at one focus, and that the line joining it to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times; a third law, linking the planets’ periods to their distances from the Sun, followed in 1619. In 1609 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a>, professor of mathematics at Padua, built a telescope after reports of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> invention, and in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidereus-nuncius">Starry Messenger</a> of 1610 he announced mountains on the Moon, stars invisible to the naked eye and four moons circling Jupiter, which he named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilean-moons">Medicean Stars</a>. His observation of the phases of Venus soon afterwards ruled out Ptolemy’s arrangement of the planets, though not Tycho’s compromise, in which the planets circle the Sun and the Sun circles the Earth. In 1616 the Church’s Congregation of the Index suspended Copernicus’s book until corrected, and in 1633, after Galileo had argued for the Earth’s motion in his Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems (1632), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a> forced him to abjure and confined him to his house near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><p>In 1628 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-harvey">William Harvey</a>, physician to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a>, showed by experiment and calculation that the blood circulates, pumped by the heart. In 1687 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>’s Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a>, showed that the same three laws of motion and a single force of universal gravitation explained the fall of bodies on Earth, the orbits of the Moon and planets, the tides and the paths of comets.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei.jpg" alt="Galileo Galilei" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galileo_Galilei_(1564-1642)_RMG_BHC2700.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Justus Sustermans, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The leading figures came from many walks of life. Copernicus (1473–1543) was a churchman, physician and administrator who let his great book be printed only at the end of his life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a> (1546–1601) was a Danish nobleman who built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a> with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-denmark">Frederick II of Denmark</a> and died as imperial mathematician to Rudolf II in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. Kepler (1571–1630), a Lutheran mathematician who served the same emperor, also had to defend his mother in a witchcraft trial. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> (1564–1642) taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> before becoming mathematician and philosopher to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-ii-de-medici">Cosimo II de' Medici</a>, grand duke of Tuscany. Descartes (1596–1650), a French Catholic, did most of his work in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. Boyle (1627–1691) was a son of the Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, and Newton (1643–1727), born into a farming family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a>, became professor of mathematics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Master of the Royal Mint and President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>.</p><p>Around them worked a much larger community. Marin Mersenne, a friar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, passed news and problems between scholars across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by letter; Henry Oldenburg, one of the Royal Society’s first secretaries, turned such correspondence into the Philosophical Transactions from 1665. Evangelista Torricelli’s barometer of 1643 and the experiment on the Puy de Dôme that Blaise Pascal arranged in 1648 showed that the air has weight, pressing less on a mountain top than at its foot, and Boyle, whose assistant Robert Hooke built him an air pump to study the “spring” of the air, published in 1662 the law of gases that bears his name. Hooke’s Micrographia (1665) and the letters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek">Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> opened the world of the microscope; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a> designed the first pendulum clock and explained the ring of Saturn; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a> developed the calculus independently of Newton and published it first, in 1684. Instrument makers, lens grinders, engravers, printers and paid assistants made much of this work possible, though their names were seldom recorded.</p><p>A few women took part despite their exclusion from universities and academies. Maria Cunitz of Silesia published Urania propitia (1650), which simplified the calculations of Kepler’s tables, and Margaret Cavendish, Duchess of Newcastle, a sharp critic of the experimental philosophy, became in 1667 the first woman to attend a meeting of the Royal Society. Much natural knowledge also came from beyond Europe through trade and empire: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hortus-malabaricus">Hortus Malabaricus</a>, a twelve-volume <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> flora of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a> published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> from 1678, rested on the knowledge of the Malayali physician Itty Achudan and other local experts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton.jpg" alt="Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newton%27s_Principia_title_page.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Zhaladshar at English Wikisource., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1700 most leading astronomers accepted that the Earth moves around the Sun, and Newton’s physics, united with Kepler’s laws, explained the motions of the heavens and of bodies on Earth by the same mathematical principles. Natural philosophers had gained new instruments, among them the telescope, microscope, barometer, thermometer, air pump and pendulum clock; new mathematics, from John Napier’s logarithms of 1614 and Descartes’s coordinate geometry of 1637 to the calculus; and new institutions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accademia-del-cimento">Accademia del Cimento</a> met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1657 to 1667; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1660 and chartered by Charles II in 1662; the Académie des sciences was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1666 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>’s minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a>; and the observatories of Paris (1667) and Greenwich (1675) and the first scientific journals followed.</p><p>The achievements were uneven. Physics and astronomy were transformed, but medicine changed slowly: Harvey’s discovery did little at first for the treatment of the sick, and bleeding remained a common remedy. Chemistry still lay between alchemy and the new philosophy, and several of the movement’s leaders, Boyle and Newton among them, studied alchemy and prophecy alongside mathematics. The universities went on teaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> for decades, and direct evidence that the Earth moves came only later, with James Bradley’s discovery of the aberration of starlight in 1729 and Friedrich Bessel’s measurement of a star’s parallax in 1838.</p><p>The Church’s ban on Copernican books was relaxed only gradually: the general prohibition was dropped in 1758, and Copernicus’s book and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a>’s Dialogue left the Index of Prohibited Books in 1835; in 1992 Pope John Paul II acknowledged the errors of Galileo’s judges. Long before that, Newton’s work had become the model of knowledge for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Age of Enlightenment</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> popularised it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet">Émilie du Châtelet</a> translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> into French, and the confidence that reason and experiment could uncover the laws of nature, and perhaps of society, passed into the 18th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment.jpg" alt="Age of Enlightenment" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Age of Enlightenment</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Les_salons_au_XVIIIe_si%C3%A8cle_-_Histoire_Image.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anicet Charles Gabriel Lemonnier, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Scientific Revolution is often seen as the origin of modern science: of the experimental method, the mathematical laws of physics, the scientific society and journal, and the expectation that knowledge of nature will keep advancing. It replaced a closed, ordered cosmos centred on the Earth with a vast universe governed everywhere by the same laws, and the image of nature as a machine, working like a clock, shaped philosophy, theology and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>’s hopes of progress.</p><p>The name itself has a history. It was given currency by the historian of science Alexandre Koyré in the late 1930s and by the English historian Herbert Butterfield, whose The Origins of Modern Science (1949) ranked the revolution above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in importance. Thomas Kuhn, author of The Copernican Revolution (1957), went on in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962) to make revolutions, shifts of “paradigm”, a general theory of how science changes. Later historians have questioned whether there was a single revolution at all; Steven Shapin began his book The Scientific Revolution (1996) with the words “There was no such thing as the Scientific Revolution, and this is a book about it.” Some stress continuity with medieval natural philosophy, as the French physicist Pierre Duhem argued in the early 20th century; others stress the slow and uneven pace of change, its roots in the workshops of craftsmen and its ties to commerce, empire and religion.</p><p>Historians also set it in a wider world. Its astronomy and optics rested on work in Greek and Arabic, and its natural history on knowledge gathered far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Joseph Needham’s studies of Chinese science and technology asked why a comparable revolution did not take place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, a question much debated and by some historians rejected as wrongly posed. Most now see the Scientific Revolution as a European transformation that drew on the knowledge of many cultures and, through Europe’s expansion, came to shape the science of the whole world.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Scientific Revolution, conventionally dated from Copernicus’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">On the Revolutions</a> (1543) to Newton’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> (1687), replaced the Earth-centred universe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>’s physics with a Sun-centred cosmos governed by universal mathematical laws. Built on Greek, Islamic and medieval foundations, it created the experimental method and the scientific society, and became the model of knowledge for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> and for modern science.</em></p>

